{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VA1eujivBiuJWXRmNVGCjH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PsKFNnZq89m3pdupyyut27","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"How Stable Are LLM Benchmarks? Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models Reveals 31% Temporal Item Instability\"\n\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)  \n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + AC Roundtable)  \n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis from psychometrics to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding that 31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This finding is replicated across six benchmarks (C% = 17.9–31.3%, all ≥45× above random controls). A Simpson's paradox analysis shows the naïve positive association between domain-level accuracy improvement and DIF (OR = 1.55) reverses after controlling for item properties (OR = 0.73), confirming instability is item-specific (≤7.6% explained by domain improvement). The paper demonstrates that web-overlap contamination labels are orthogonal to DIF (enrichment ≈ 1.0), proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR = 0.000), and notes that despite 31% instability, model rankings remain highly stable (ρ ≈ 0.997).\n\n**Four claimed contributions:**\n1. Empirical discovery at scale (31.3% temporal DIF, 6 benchmarks, item-specific)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap orthogonal to DIF)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework (ΔTPR + cross-subject matching)\n4. DIF audit protocol for benchmark maintainers\n\n---\n\n## 2. Converged Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Converged | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 | 4.0 | **4.0** | Controls are above norm for NLP empirical papers; MH-DIF is correctly applied; causal ambiguity is acknowledged honestly |\n| **Excitement** | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** | Important finding for LLM evaluation, constrained by off-the-shelf method and missing detector comparison |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.0 | 4.5 | **3.5** | Method fully specified on public data (METABENCH), but no code release mentioned |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** | Strong Findings candidate; detector comparison gap caps below Main threshold |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | 4 | **4** | Both reviewers independently converge on most points |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consensus Strengths\n\n**S1. Impressive scale and thoroughness of controls.** 5,227 models × 12,508 items across 6 benchmarks. The control structure — random-split (0.18%), permutation null (0.21% ± 0.09%, >345σ), ability-quintile (2.9%), within-family comparison, nearest-neighbor matching, BH-FDR correction, threshold sensitivity (C% at |Δ_MH| ≥ 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5) — is among the most thorough in LLM evaluation literature. Both reviewers independently called this out as a major strength.\n\n**S2. Cross-benchmark replication.** The 6-benchmark validation (MMLU 31.3%, WinoGrande 30.3%, TruthfulQA 29.7%, ARC-Challenge 26.9%, GSM8K 18.9%, HellaSwag 17.9%) upgrades the paper from \"interesting MMLU analysis\" to \"generalizable finding about LLM benchmarks as a class.\" All random controls < 1%, minimum enrichment 45×. This is the single result that most strengthens the paper.\n\n**S3. Simpson's paradox analysis.** The OR reversal (1.55 → 0.73) after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination is a genuinely insightful finding. It convincingly demonstrates that domain-level accuracy improvement does not drive DIF, and that 96% of DIF variance is item-specific. The LR test (χ² = 647, p ≈ 0) confirms item properties are genuine confounders. This is the paper's most memorable analytical contribution.\n\n**S4. Cross-subject matching innovation.** Replacing within-subject total score with cross-subject total score as the MH matching variable eliminates matching variable contamination (ΔFPR = 0.000 across all conditions, vs. +0.186 FPR inflation with standard matching). This is a practical methodological contribution with clear psychometric grounding (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue & Allen 1993).\n\n**S5. Global-vs-differential contamination distinction.** The conceptual contribution that web-overlap labels measure global exposure while DIF measures differential functioning — and the empirical confirmation (enrichment = 0.96, Jaccard = 0.206) — reframes how the field should think about validation of contamination detectors.\n\n**S6. Honest treatment of limitations.** The paper proactively addresses IRT misfit (98% |Lz| > 2, explained by MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality; MH-DIF is nonparametric), RL robustness (only 13 reasoning-RL models), causal ambiguity, temporal coarseness, and benchmark scope.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Consensus Weaknesses\n\n### CW1 (Major): No comparison with existing contamination detectors\nBoth reviewers independently identify this as the single largest gap. The paper discusses Min-K%, ConStat, DVD, and Self-Critique in Related Work (§2) but never runs any of them on the same items. The experiment plan included ConStat/DVD experiments (plan_003a/b/c) that were dropped when the paper pivoted from \"detection\" to \"characterization\" framing. While the pivot is defensible, readers will inevitably ask: \"How do DIF-flagged items relate to items flagged by behavioral detectors?\" The enrichment ≈ 1.0 against web-overlap labels is an indirect comparison, but web-overlap is not a behavioral detector. Even a small-scale comparison on a subset of items would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n**Impact on score:** This is the primary factor keeping the paper at 3.5 rather than 4.0. \n**Fixable in revision:** Yes, at least partially.\n\n### CW2 (Major): Causal ambiguity\nMH-DIF detects *that* items function differently across cohorts but cannot determine *why*. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but occasionally slides into causal language in the main text (e.g., semi-synthetic \"contamination\" injection, \"exploitation rate,\" the abstract's framing). If DIF cannot distinguish contamination from genuine capability change, the practical implications for benchmark maintenance are unclear: should flagged items be removed as contaminated, or retained as capturing real capability evolution? The \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing helps but does not fully resolve this.\n\n**Impact on score:** Limits Excitement. This is a fundamental limitation of the MH-DIF approach, not a paper flaw.\n**Fixable in revision:** Partially — language can be made more consistently hedged, but the core ambiguity is inherent.\n\n### CW3 (Moderate): Temporal cohort definition is coarse\nThe 2023-vs-2024-H1 binary split aggregates models with vastly different training pipelines, data mixtures, and scales. The 99.98% decoder-only composition addresses architecture, but training methodology (SFT vs DPO vs GRPO) and scale are potential confounds. The ability-matched analysis (quintile C% = 2.9%) mitigates but does not eliminate this. Item-Focused Trees (81% concordance) provide partial validation. Finer-grained temporal analysis would strengthen the paper.\n\n**Fixable in revision:** Partially, constrained by metadata availability.\n\n### CW4 (Moderate): No code release\nThe analysis uses publicly available data (METABENCH) and a standard psychometric procedure (MH-DIF). All parameters are specified (K = 5 strata, ETS thresholds, BH-FDR q = 0.05). However, no code repository is mentioned. For a paper proposing an audit protocol, releasing the implementation would significantly increase practical impact and reproducibility.\n\n**Fixable in revision:** Yes, trivially.\n\n### CW5 (Moderate): Semi-synthetic framing tension\nThe paper positions itself as \"characterization not detection\" but reports TPR/FPR metrics from injection experiments. This creates internal tension. The resolution is that semi-synthetic experiments validate method sensitivity, not detection accuracy — but this distinction is not clearly drawn in the text. The uniform injection mechanism (flipping incorrect→correct at rate p) is also a simplification of real contamination dynamics.\n\n**Fixable in revision:** Yes, with clearer framing language.\n\n### CW6 (Moderate): Audit protocol underdeveloped\nClaim 4 promises \"a concrete DIF audit protocol\" but §4.6 delivers three sentences of recommendations (quarterly audits, C% threshold, graduated responses). This is a sketch, not a protocol. Either develop it with worked examples and operationalization details, or demote it from a numbered contribution.\n\n**Fixable in revision:** Yes.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Minor Issues\n\n- **ΔFPR = 0.000 with CI [0.000, 0.000]:** If this is an algebraic guarantee (which it appears to be), state it as a theorem. If empirical, report to more decimal places.\n- **z-scores (−8 to −49σ) for discriminative signal concentration:** Clarify the reference distribution generating these values.\n- **Missing CI on headline 31.3% C%:** A bootstrap CI would be informative, though the threshold sensitivity and cross-benchmark replication serve a similar purpose.\n- **Abstract density:** Too many parenthetical statistics for a first read. Consider prioritizing the 2–3 most important numbers.\n- **ρ ≈ 0.997 may mask individual differences:** 25% of models shift by ≥100 ranks. For pairwise model comparisons (rather than aggregate leaderboard), instability could be practically significant.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing the paper against the full experiment plan:\n\n| Experiment | Status in Paper | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| plan_001: MMLU temporal DIF | Table 1 (core result) | ✅ Fully covered |\n| plan_028: 6-benchmark cross-validation | Table 2 | ✅ Fully covered |\n| plan_030: Simpson's paradox regression | Table 3 | ✅ Fully covered |\n| plan_011: Semi-synthetic validation | Table 4 | ✅ Fully covered |\n| plan_029: Permutation null | Text (§4.2) | ✅ Covered |\n| plan_010: Architecture stratification | Text (§4.2) | ✅ Covered |\n| plan_008: Graceful degradation | §4.6 | ✅ Covered |\n| plan_009: Item-Focused Trees | §4.6 | ✅ Covered |\n| plan_004: GSM8K semi-synthetic | §4.5 | ✅ Covered |\n| plan_002: RL robustness | Limitations | ⚠️ Honest disclosure, not executed |\n| plan_007: Cumulative Bayesian DIF | Not visible | ⚠️ May be appendix-only |\n| plan_003a/b/c: ConStat/DVD/ensemble | Dropped | ❌ Correctly dropped for framing but creates CW1 gap |\n| plan_005: Bifactor IRT equating | Limitations mention | ✅ Correctly killed, mentioned |\n| plan_006: Item generation | Not included | ✅ Correctly dropped |\n\nThe paper covers 9/14 planned experiments in the main text, with appropriate handling of dropped/killed experiments. The main coverage gap is plan_003a/b/c (detector comparison), which is also the consensus #1 weakness.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you run any existing contamination detector (Min-K%, ConStat) on the same items, even on a subset? What is the overlap with DIF flags?\n2. Is ΔFPR = 0.000 an algebraic guarantee of the cross-subject matching design, or an empirical observation? If algebraic, can you provide a proof sketch?\n3. What happens to C% with finer temporal granularity (quarterly splits)?\n4. Can you characterize which items show DIF beyond difficulty and discrimination? Are there content-level features (topic recency, question format) that predict DIF?\n5. The cumulative Bayesian DIF analysis (plan_007) — is this in the appendix? If so, what are the power accumulation curves?\n\n---\n\n## 8. Reviewer-Specific Notes\n\n### R1 (Methods + Experiments)\n- MH-DIF correctly applied with appropriate parameterization\n- Cross-subject matching is the strongest methodological contribution\n- Semi-synthetic injection is valid but simplified; non-uniform injection profiles would strengthen\n- Statistical reporting is above average (effect sizes + CIs + p-values consistently)\n- The BH-FDR removing only 3/3918 items is expected due to ETS dual threshold (effect size + significance), not a concern\n\n### R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n- Novelty is moderate: empirical application of off-the-shelf method elevated by Simpson's paradox analysis, cross-subject matching, and ground truth meta-critique\n- Related work is comprehensive (~35 references across 4 clusters); DIF foundational references are adequate\n- The \"characterization not detection\" framing mostly holds but TPR/FPR reporting creates tension\n- \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" is well-executed but title/text occasionally pull in opposite directions\n- Blebea et al. (2024) is cited in the paper (§2, IRT cluster); differentiated by focus on temporal measurement invariance vs. static item quality\n\n---\n\n## 9. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3.5 (Borderline Conference / Strong Findings)**\n\nThis is a solid empirical contribution that applies established psychometric methods (MH-DIF) to LLM benchmarks at impressive scale, with unusually thorough controls and genuine analytical insight. The 31% temporal instability finding, replicated across 6 benchmarks, is an important result for the LLM evaluation community. The Simpson's paradox analysis (OR 1.55 → 0.73) and cross-subject matching (ΔFPR = 0.000) are the paper's strongest contributions beyond the headline finding.\n\nTwo factors cap the paper below 4.0 (Conference): (1) the absence of any empirical comparison with existing contamination detectors, which is the most natural question any reader will ask; and (2) the novelty is primarily empirical application of a known psychometric method, with methodological contributions being adaptations rather than innovations.\n\n**Champion potential:** A reviewer could credibly advocate for 4.0 if (a) the authors add even a small-scale detector comparison (showing orthogonality or complementarity), (b) code is released, and (c) causal language in the abstract is softened. The paper is close to the threshold.\n\n**Recommendation:** Accept as Findings. A revision addressing the detector comparison gap (CW1) and code release (CW4) could move this to Conference acceptance.","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260521T110541","overall_score":"3.5 — Borderline Conference / Strong Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1202,"cache_read_input_tokens":69371,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1202},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"08c58412-17e2-4f74-8c66-bef7dc33333c","uuid":"ff77c449-bf90-47a0-8e86-cb9df54f25ee"}
